1
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:09,360
A juice company says: 9 out of 10 people prefer our juice!

2
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:12,220
The survey was done outside its own shop.

3
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:16,250
It asked: don't you agree our juice tastes better?

4
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:21,510
What are the main problems: a biased sample and a leading question, or nothing?

5
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:24,700
Pause the video and choose.

6
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:28,390
A biased sample, because they asked its own customers.

7
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:33,240
And a leading question, which pushes people towards one answer.

8
00:00:33,290 --> 00:00:36,820
Remember: a sample should represent the population.

9
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:39,680
A sample that does not is biased.

10
00:00:39,730 --> 00:00:41,900
Bias makes results lean one way.

11
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:44,130
Here are common sources.

12
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:48,420
Non-response: only some people reply, and they may be different.

13
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:53,000
Time and place: a traffic count on a holiday is not typical.

14
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:54,680
The wording matters too.

15
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:59,340
A question that pushes people towards one answer is biased.

16
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:00,010
Your turn.

17
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:04,760
'Don't you agree …?' Is that a leading question, or a neutral question?

18
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:07,960
Pause the video and choose.

19
00:01:08,010 --> 00:01:09,650
It is a leading question.

20
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:16,210
A neutral question gives fair choices: which juice do you prefer, A, B, or no preference?

21
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:26,070
To avoid bias: use a random or stratified sample of a sensible size, ask neutral questions, and collect data at typical times and places.

22
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,440
Here is the method.

23
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:29,030
Step 1: who was asked?

24
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,530
Do they represent everyone?

25
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:34,280
Step 2: how many were asked?

26
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:37,010
Step 3: how were the questions worded?

27
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,640
When and where?

28
00:01:38,690 --> 00:01:41,910
Step 4: are the results shown fairly?

29
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:45,780
A survey about public transport is done at a bus station.

30
00:01:45,830 --> 00:01:49,600
Bus users are over-represented, so it is biased.

31
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:54,380
Counting cars at 3 in the morning to plan for traffic is also biased.

32
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:57,080
Count at several typical times.

33
00:01:57,130 --> 00:01:58,930
Now look at a bar chart.

34
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:01,860
Its vertical axis starts at 95, not 0.

35
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:05,950
A value of 98 looks three times as tall as 96.

36
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:06,640
Your turn.

37
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:12,470
A vertical axis that does not start at 0: can it be misleading, or is it always fine?

38
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:15,040
Pause and choose.

39
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:16,500
It can be misleading.

40
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:20,540
The axis should start at 0, or show a clear break.

41
00:02:20,590 --> 00:02:26,250
A poll on a gaming website gets 50,000 votes on: should schools ban games?

42
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:27,330
Zara comments.

43
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:29,420
Which line is wrong?

44
00:02:29,470 --> 00:02:30,520
Line 1 is wrong.

45
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:32,090
Size does not fix bias.

46
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:37,780
The voters are gamers who chose to vote, so they do not represent everyone.

47
00:02:37,830 --> 00:02:41,390
Large but unrepresentative is still biased.

48
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:46,660
Adverts and news reports sometimes use biased samples or misleading graphs.

49
00:02:46,710 --> 00:02:49,880
Always ask how the data was collected.

50
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:51,250
One last question.

51
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:55,820
A questionnaire is posted to 1,000 homes, and only 80 reply.

52
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:59,700
What does that risk: non-response bias, or no bias?

53
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:03,150
Pause the video and choose.

54
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:04,410
Non-response bias.

55
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:09,150
The people who reply may be different from those who do not.

56
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:15,910
To remember: bias comes from unfair samples, leading questions, timing and non-response.

57
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:19,220
Use fair samples and neutral questions.

58
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:23,120
Check graphs and claims for missing information.

59
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:25,880
A big sample can still be biased.

60
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:30,990
Great work! Now practise lesson 6.2 on Camday.
